Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 07:21:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(evidence): make every claim carry its literature,
 and let the evidence set the grammar
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Faz 34, Bölüm A. The scientific layer the recommendation surface will sit on.

The repository had thirty engines and five cited boundaries, with exactly two
year-citations across all engine code. PrescriptionEngine's own header sets the
standard this change is trying to meet — "a suggestion the user cannot interrogate
is a horoscope" — and until now nothing behind it was checkable.

Reference replaces free-text citation with a type that carries a locator, the
sample the finding was established in, and a mandatory statement of what the study
does *not* show. That last field cannot be empty: writing what a paper fails to
establish takes more thought than writing what it establishes, and it cuts
overclaiming off at the source rather than at the sentence.

ClaimStrength derives the grammar a sentence may use from the evidence behind it.
Weak support cannot reach the imperative, a claim resting on sources that
contradict each other drops a step, and a claim resting on an unconfirmed source
can never become a recommendation. An instruction to "avoid overclaiming" survives
review and then erodes over a hundred copy edits; a failing test does not.

PopulationTransfer prices the distance between a study's sample and the person
reading the screen. Most health software resolves that gap by not mentioning it.

Four of the sixteen sources are flagged needsVerification — findings that are well
established but whose exact bibliographic details could not be confirmed without a
network. They still inform the app; they just cannot license the imperative.
docs/EVIDENCE.md lists them separately so they can be cleared by hand.

RecommendationEngine computes no physiology. It reads what the existing engines
already produced and decides what the app is entitled to say about it. Much of it
lands on .observation, and the acute:chronic workload card is the clearest case:
the ratio is widely used, the objection that its numerator sits inside its
denominator is unanswered, and the two positions are recorded as contradicting
each other. The app reports the number and declines to instruct on it.

Also here:
- Scripts/check-citations.py, wired as preflight's seventh pass. It generates
  docs/EVIDENCE.md so the document cannot drift from the table. Negative-tested
  against six deliberate breakages; all six were caught.
- It found a real one on first run: a free-text Minetti citation in EnduranceEngine
  with no boundary behind it. Now ENDURANCE-1, carrying the detail a bare
  author-and-year in a comment never does — ten trained men, on a treadmill.
- The first version of the grammar check matched by prefix and flagged the app's
  own prose: "ölçütün" begins with "ölç". It now matches whole words plus the
  Turkish imperative endings, which also gets the linguistics right in the other
  direction — an infinitive inside a hedge is not an instruction.

No existing score changes value, and no existing test signature moves: every new
field is defaulted and DecisionInput is untouched.

Not compiled — this environment has no Swift toolchain. All seven preflight passes
are clean, including the cross-target type and named-argument resolution checks.

