 ladder
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The page that was here was a link stub — a title and two buttons — and it used
a palette (#090A0F / #38BDF8) that belongs to nothing else in the project. The
app's real identity lives in ZenithiumColor and docs/design-direction.html:
ink #07090E, teal #3ED0BE, Archivo over IBM Plex. This page uses that one.

The hero is not a phone mockup. It is the thing no competitor's page can show,
because no competitor has it: the same morning, read three ways, as the evidence
behind each reading weakens. A consensus statement earns the imperative. Plews
2013 does not, because that paper argues for weekly averages and reading a single
night as a verdict would be citing it against itself. The load ratio gets no
instruction at all, because Gabbett and Lolli disagree and the app says so.

Every citation, confidence figure and "what this does not show" line on the page
is copied from EvidenceLibrary rather than written for the page — so the site
cannot drift from what the app actually says.

The refusals section advertises what the app will not do: no diagnosis, no
calorie tracking, no supplement advice, no chat model, no social feed. On a
health app that reads as confidence rather than apology, and each line is a real
constraint enforced in the codebase.

privacy.html and support.html still carry the old stub palette and will now look
unrelated to the page linking to them. Left alone deliberately — those two are
App Store compliance surfaces and are not mine to restyle without asking.

